at individual needs to be  pardoned well within his authority the  cases that had not even gone to trial  yet so again I think the president just  needs to be cautious about signaling  what he thinks the appropriate outcome  of those investigations should be though  generally speaking do you think it's a  good idea after they've already been  convicted I know he has Authority he can  do whatever he wants but generally  speaking do you think it's a good idea  well again I'd be a little reluctant to  talk about it  oddly I think I'd have to look at each  individual case president Trump tweeted  yesterday if Iran wants to fight that  will be the official end of Iran are you  concerned we could be headed to a major  conference yes I'm not as concerned as I  think a lot of people are the president  doesn't want to go to war in Iran the  Iranians certainly don't want to go to  war with us  I haven't seen the intelligence that is  kind of kind of predicating all of this  but the fact the matter is if there are  threats out there we know how to deal  with those threats we know how to  elevate our security status and trust me  the United States Navy has been dealing  with the Iranians in the Persian Gulf  for 4050 years they will take care of  the fleet and they didn't the Iranians  do not want to engage us in the Persian  Gulf that will not end well for them do  you mind if we turn to see stories no  ma'am I want to talk about the Osama bin  Laden chapter because it for me it was  one of the most detailed things I've  ever read about what happened with Osama  bin Laden you were part of the team who  briefed the president you certainly saw  the seals right before they went on that  mission and what did you want us to know  because it was you took us on a step by  step very harrowing story what's your  big takeaway for us in that yeah you  know I offer the takeaway is while you  know the seals and the night stalkers  the helicopter pilots that participated  in the raid we were honored and  fortunate enough to be the kind of the  final piece of this this very  challenging puzzle at the end of the day  it was about the hundreds of thousands  of soldiers sailors airmen Marines  civilians the CIA the NSA everybody that  contributed to bringing bin Laden to  justice I know that Admiral but at the  end of the day you're there as the seals  are getting their headsets on they're  playing music they're getting ready to  go in and do this and there were two  occasions on that night where you say  you choked up yourself yes Oshin well  again it's hard not to get emotional  when you know you've spent your you know  most of your adult life trying to bring  justice after 9/11 so you know certainly  when the when the guys got back across  the border and I knew they were safe and  I for all intent and purpose the mission  was over yeah this there's a certain  emotional relief that happens as a  result of were you at all nervous that  night you know I wasn't all that nervous  I had kind of hand selected the  commander's I knew what the capability  of the guys were I knew that we could do  this mission yeah an obviously we didn't  know where the bin Laden was there and  and I go back to the decision that  President Obama made an incredibly  courageous decision to go in when  frankly it was about a 50/50 chance that  he was there yeah and and the work that  the CIA did and Leon Panetta and I think  that will go down as one of the the  great intelligence operations in the  history of American intelligence rigor  and courage involved I do want to get to  one thing there's a generation following  yours the millennial generation not  known for rigor and courage overall but  you say in fact you think they will be  the greatest generation of the 21st  century I do tell me why well I think it  would be surprised I am probably the  biggest fan of the Millennials you'll  ever meet and you know I always Sharon  let's maligned the much maligned  Millennials yeah and